From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oliver Sargison <sarol263@student.otago.ac.nz>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 20:17:33 +1300
Subject: [PATCH 28/52] Plutonium: shm buffers the GPU can't import don't stall
 the display; EXO-DIAG logs at VLOG(1)

EXO-IMPORT: creating a shared image the GPU process can't back stops the
client's whole GPU channel (SharedImageStub::OnError()). For Exo that
client is the browser, so every context was lost, the display
compositor's too, and Exo tried the same import again at the app's next
commit. On X11-hosted sessions (ANGLE on Vulkan) a Qt app's XRGB8888
wl_shm buffers (BGRX_8888) did this, and stalled every window.

The GPU process now reports the 32-bit RGB formats it can import from
shared memory (SharedImageCapabilities::shared_memory_import_formats),
found as SharedImageFactory::CreateSharedImage() would: a factory that
takes the buffer, or a GPU backing for a CompoundImageBacking. wl_shm
imports an X format it can't as its A twin, with the alpha byte as
padding (Buffer::alpha_is_padding(), so the surface stays opaque); a
format with no importable twin isn't advertised and is refused at
create_buffer with invalid_format. Buffer::Texture::Create() drops any
other unimportable shm buffer before it reaches the GPU process. An
empty list (a GPU process that doesn't say) changes nothing.

The EXO-DIAG logging from the overlay viability work, LOG(ERROR) on hot
paths, is now VLOG(1).
